---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
const recentNotes=(await getCollection("notes",({data})=>!data.draft)).sort((a,b)=>b.data.published.valueOf()-a.data.published.valueOf()).slice(0,2);
---
<BaseLayout><main id="top">
<header class="site-header shell"><a class="wordmark" href="#top" aria-label="Jesse Anderson, home">JA<span>.</span></a><nav aria-label="Primary navigation"><a href="#work">Work</a><a href="#notes">Notes</a><a href="#about">About</a></nav><a class="header-link" href="https://github.com/jessecanderson">GitHub ↗</a></header>
<section class="hero shell"><div class="hero-index mono">PORTFOLIO / 2026</div><div class="hero-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow"><span></span>Jesse Anderson · Software Engineer</p><h1>I build software<br><em>to understand</em><br>what comes next.</h1></div><div class="hero-aside"><p>Software engineer at The Walt Disney Company with a background spanning infrastructure, cloud architecture, and product development. Now focused on AI tooling and developer experience.</p><a class="text-link" href="#work">See what I’m building ↓</a></div></div><div class="hero-rule"></div></section>
<section class="work shell" id="work"><div class="section-head"><div><p class="mono section-number">01 / SELECTED WORK</p><h2>Things worth<br>building.</h2></div><p class="section-intro">Two ambitious products, built in the open and improved through real use.</p></div><div class="projects">
<article class="project"><div class="project-top"><span class="mono">/01</span><span class="status"><i></i>Actively building</span></div><div class="project-visual terminal"><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jessecanderson/cyberdeck/main/docs/screenshots/agent-command.svg" alt="Cyberdeck agent command center interface"></div><p class="project-kicker mono">Local AI agent command center</p><h3>Cyberdeck</h3><p class="project-copy">A neon, keyboard-first terminal interface for running multiple local coding agents. Built around fast context switching, visible permission boundaries, and a shared runtime that can grow with new providers and modules.</p><ul class="tags" aria-label="Cyberdeck technologies"><li>Python</li><li>Textual</li><li>Codex</li><li>ACP</li><li>Open source</li></ul><a class="project-link" href="https://github.com/jessecanderson/cyberdeck">View the project <span>↗</span></a></article>
<article class="project"><div class="project-top"><span class="mono">/02</span><span class="status"><i></i>In development</span></div><div class="project-visual map" aria-hidden="true"><div class="contour"></div><div class="route"></div><b class="pin a">●</b><b class="pin b">●</b><span class="map-label one">RED RIVER GORGE</span><span class="map-label two">CAMP / 04</span></div><p class="project-kicker mono">The whole climbing trip, in one place</p><h3>ClimbSite</h3><p class="project-copy">A climber-focused trip planner connecting areas, nearby camping, approach notes, parking friction, and trusted source links. Starting with the Red River Gorge and designed to complement—not replace—guidebooks.</p><ul class="tags" aria-label="ClimbSite technologies"><li>Next.js</li><li>Postgres</li><li>Maps</li><li>Auth.js</li><li>Product design</li></ul><a class="project-link" href="https://github.com/jessecanderson/ClimbSite">View the project <span>↗</span></a></article>
</div></section>
<section class="notes shell" id="notes"><div class="section-head"><div><p class="mono section-number">02 / FIELD NOTES</p><h2>Learning in public.</h2></div><p class="section-intro">Working notes on AI, product decisions, and the messy middle of making things.</p></div><div class="note-list">{recentNotes.map((note,index)=><a class="note" href={`/notes/${note.id.replace(/\.(md|mdx)$/,"")}/`}><span class="mono">{note.data.published.toLocaleDateString("en-US",{month:"short",year:"numeric"}).toUpperCase()}</span><div><small>FIELD NOTE</small><h3>{note.data.title}</h3></div><b>{String(index+1).padStart(2,"0")}</b></a>)}<a class="note" href="/notes/"><span class="mono">ALL NOTES</span><div><small>WRITING</small><h3>Browse the Field Notes</h3></div><b>↗</b></a><a class="note" href="/archive/"><span class="mono">ARCHIVE</span><div><small>2014—2022</small><h3>Earlier notes, projects, and ideas</h3></div><b>↗</b></a></div></section>
<section class="about shell" id="about"><p class="mono section-number">03 / ABOUT</p><div class="about-grid"><div><h2>Curious by default.<br><em>Shipping on purpose.</em></h2><ul class="professional-snapshot" aria-label="Professional snapshot"><li><span>2007</span> Technology career began</li><li><span>2018</span> Professional software development</li><li><span>AWS</span> Former Solutions Architect</li><li><span>NOW</span> AI tooling & developer experience</li></ul></div><div><p>I’m Jesse Anderson, a software engineer at The Walt Disney Company. I’ve worked in technology since 2007, beginning in infrastructure and systems administration. Along the way, I spent a year as a Solutions Architect at AWS, building a deeper understanding of cloud architecture and how complex systems fit together.</p><p>I’ve been developing software professionally since 2018, but my path into development was largely self-taught. That background shapes how I work: understand the system beneath the abstraction, follow how the pieces connect, and keep building until an idea becomes useful.</p><p>Today, I’m focused on AI tooling and developer experience—building Cyberdeck to explore multi-agent workflows, developing ClimbSite as a practical product, and documenting what I learn along the way.</p><a class="text-link" href="mailto:hello@jesseanderson.dev">Start a conversation ↗</a></div></div></section>
<footer class="shell"><div class="footer-mark">JA<span>.</span></div><p>Building, learning, documenting.</p><div><a href="https://github.com/jessecanderson">GitHub</a><a href="mailto:hello@jessecanderson.dev">Email</a><a href="#top">Top ↑</a></div></footer>
</main></BaseLayout>
